                <pb facs="0795" n="787" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg787"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611b.9417/226: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Donovan</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-02-16T12:00:00-05:00">February 16,
                        1937—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your February 6, 9 a.m. The position taken by you in response to the request of
                    Mr. Shotaro Kakisaki is quite correct. You may wish to inform him, however, that
                    the present problem is being discussed with the Japanese Embassy here, and that
                    the Japanese Government will be informed of any means by which this Government
                    can cooperate in securing the desired information for the Japanese
                    exporters.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
